{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of volatility: The model consistently forecasts a flat or slowly trending path, failing to capture the magnitude of price spikes (e.g., rising to ~76) and subsequent sharp drops (falling to ~66). Apply a volatility multiplier to the forecast range.",
    "Address systematic lag in trend reversal: The model reacts too slowly to sentiment shifts, often predicting a continuation of the previous trend while the true price has already reversed. When sentiment history shows a sequence of 1.00s followed by 0.00s (or vice versa), adjust the forecast immediately to reflect the new regime rather than smoothing the transition.",
    "Implement mean reversion after spikes: The model fails to anticipate the rapid pullback following a price peak. If the forecast predicts a continued rise or stability after a true outcome spike, force a downward correction in the subsequent 5-10 days to align with the observed mean-reverting behavior.",
    "Decouple sentiment from linear projection: High sentiment scores (1.00) do not correlate with the model's linear upward drift in the examples provided. Do not rely on sentiment history to drive the slope of the forecast; instead, use sentiment to trigger regime switches (e.g., from 'stable' to 'volatile') and adjust the forecast variance accordingly."
  ]
}
